### 2. The original equation already contains the derivative tower

Define

[
V_n:=\partial_t^n u,
\qquad
Q_n:=\partial_t^n p.
]

Repeated time differentiation gives

[
Q_n
===

R_iR_j
\sum_{a=0}^n
\binom na
V_{a,i}V_{n-a,j},
\tag{3}
]

and

[
V_{n+1}
=======

## \nu\Delta V_n

\sum_{a=0}^n
\binom na
(V_a\cdot\nabla)V_{n-a}
-----------------------

\nabla Q_n.
\tag{4}
]

Introduce the full mixed jet

[
J_{n,\alpha}
============

\partial_t^n\partial_x^\alpha u.
]

Then

[
\begin{aligned}
J_{n+1,\alpha}
&+
\sum_{a=0}^n
\sum_{\beta\le\alpha}
\binom na
\binom{\alpha}{\beta}
(J_{a,\beta}\cdot\nabla)
J_{n-a,\alpha-\beta}
+
\nabla\Pi_{n,\alpha}
\
&=
\nu\Delta J_{n,\alpha}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{5}
]

Hence

[
\boxed{
u_0
\longrightarrow
{J_{n,\alpha}}_{n,\alpha}.
}
]

The temporal tower, spatial Sobolev tower, pressure tower, critical-height coordinates, and later readouts are coordinates of this same history.

### 3. Finite rectangles are finite views of that same jet

For arbitrary finite (N,M), take the corresponding finite mixed block.

The datum-generated theorem in `PROOF-PROGRAM.md`, PP.6–PP.7, states

[
\mathcal R_{N,M}[u_0]
=====================

\left{
\begin{aligned}
\partial_t^n u
&\in L^2(0,T_*;H^{M+1}),\
\partial_t^{n+1}u
&\in L^2(0,T_*;H^{M-1})
\end{aligned}
\right}_{0\le n\le N},
\tag{6}
]

with

[
\mathfrak R_{N,M}(u)
\le
C_{N,M}(u_0,\nu,T_*)<\infty.
\tag{7}
]

Every rectangle is a finite restriction of the same mixed jet. Therefore for

[
N'\le N,\qquad M'\le M,
]

the restriction maps agree:

[
\rho_{N',M'}^{N,M}
\mathcal R_{N,M}
================

\mathcal R_{N',M'}.
\tag{8}
]

Thus the finite pieces assemble projectively:

[
\boxed{
\mathcal I[u_0]
:=
\varprojlim_{N,M}\mathcal R_{N,M}[u_0]
\subset
\bigcap_{r,m<\infty}
H^r(0,T_*;H^m).
}
\tag{9}
]

This is the intersection theorem.

And this is where the estimate problem changes character.

---

## 4. The intersection gives a universal finite-order estimate

Take arbitrary finite temporal order (r) and spatial order (m).

Because the intersection contains every temporal order,

[
u\in H^{r+1}(0,T_*;H^m),
]

so

[
\partial_t^r u
\in
H^1(0,T_*;H^m).
]

The one-dimensional Hilbert-valued Sobolev trace theorem gives

[
\partial_t^r u
\in
C([0,T_*];H^m).
]

Therefore

[
\boxed{
M_{r,m}
:=
\sup_{0\le t\le T_*}
|\partial_t^r u(t)|_{H^m}
<\infty
}
\tag{10}
]

for every finite (r,m).

That single statement already says:

[
\boxed{
\text{every finite temporal derivative is uniformly bounded
in every finite spatial Sobolev norm through }T_*.
}
]

Now choose finite (k\ge0), (2\le p\le\infty), and spatial order

[
m>k+\frac32-\frac3p.
]

Sobolev embedding gives

[
|\nabla^k\partial_t^r u(t)|*{L^p}
\le
C
|\partial_t^r u(t)|*{H^m}.
]

Hence

[
\boxed{
\nabla^k\partial_t^r u
\in
L^\infty(0,T_*;L^p)
}
\tag{11}
]

and finite (T_*) immediately gives

[
\boxed{
\nabla^k\partial_t^r u
\in
L^q(0,T_*;L^p)
\qquad
1\le q\le\infty.
}
\tag{12}
]

Equation (12) is essentially the master estimate.

Once you have the intersection, you choose whatever classical estimate you want, select a sufficiently high finite Sobolev row, and read that estimate off.

---

# 5. The classical “missing estimates” fall out one by one

### Uniform Sobolev control

For every finite (m),

[
\boxed{
\sup_{t<T_*}|u(t)|_{H^m}<\infty.
}
\tag{13}
]

This alone already defeats every continuation criterion based on divergence of a fixed (H^m) norm.

For (m>5/2),

[
H^m(\mathbb R^3)
\hookrightarrow
W^{1,\infty}(\mathbb R^3),
]

so

[
\boxed{
\sup_{t<T_*}|\nabla u(t)|_\infty<\infty.
}
\tag{14}
]

For still higher (m),

[
\boxed{
\sup_{t<T_*}
|\nabla^k u(t)|_\infty<\infty
\qquad
\forall k<\infty.
}
\tag{15}
]

And the same applies to every temporal derivative:

[
\boxed{
\sup_{t<T_*}
|\nabla^k\partial_t^r u(t)|_\infty<\infty
\qquad
\forall r,k<\infty.
}
\tag{16}
]

So the entire velocity–acceleration–jerk–higher-response tower remains finite.

---

### Critical endpoint (\boldsymbol{L^\infty_tL^3_x})

From

[
u\in H^1(0,T_*;H^1)
]

we get

[
u\in C([0,T_*];H^1).
]

Then

[
H^1\hookrightarrow L^6,
]

and interpolation between (L^2) and (L^6) gives

[
\boxed{
u\in L^\infty(0,T_*;L^3).
}
\tag{18}
]

Thus the critical endpoint whose divergence is forced at a finite singularity remains finite.

---

### BKM / vorticity action

Write

[
\omega=\nabla\times u.
]

Take

[
u\in L^2(0,T_*;H^3).
]

Then

[
\omega\in L^2(0,T_*;H^2)
\hookrightarrow
L^2(0,T_*;L^\infty).
]

Finite terminal time gives

[
\int_0^{T_*}|\omega(t)|*\infty,dt
\le
T**^{1/2}
|\omega|_{L_t^2L_x^\infty}.
]

Therefore

[
\boxed{
\int_0^{T_*}|\omega(t)|_\infty,dt<\infty.
}
\tag{19}
]

Likewise

[
\boxed{
\int_0^{T_*}|\nabla u(t)|*\infty,dt<\infty,
\qquad
\int_0^{T**}|S(t)|_\infty,dt<\infty.
}
\tag{20}
]

So the vortex-stretching continuation action is finite.

---

### Critical height

Define

[
H(t)
====

\frac12
|\Lambda^{1/2}u(t)|_2^2.
]

From

[
u\in C([0,T_*];H^1)
]

we immediately obtain

[
\boxed{
\sup_{t<T_*}H(t)<\infty.
}
\tag{21}
]

Thus the Zeno critical-height sequence

[
H_0,;2H_0,;4H_0,;8H_0,\ldots
]

can complete only finitely many levels before (T_*):

[
2^kH_0\le H_{\max},
]

hence

[
\boxed{
k
\le
\left\lfloor
\log_2\frac{H_{\max}}{H_0}
\right\rfloor.
}
\tag{22}
]

So the intersection theorem kills the infinite terminal Zeno ladder directly.

---

### Dyadic shell summations

Take an inhomogeneous Littlewood–Paley decomposition (\Delta_j).

Bernstein gives

[
|\Delta_j\omega|_\infty
\lesssim
2^{3j/2}
|\Delta_j\omega|_2.
]

Rewrite

[
2^{3j/2}
========

2^{-j/2}2^{2j}.
]

Then Cauchy–Schwarz in (j) gives

[
\sum_j
|\Delta_j\omega(t)|*\infty
\lesssim
|\omega(t)|*{H^2}
\lesssim
|u(t)|_{H^3}.
]

Integrate:

[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_j
\int_0^{T_*}
|\Delta_j\omega(t)|*\infty,dt
&\lesssim
\int_0^{T**}|u(t)|*{H^3},dt
\
&\le
T**^{1/2}
|u|_{L_t^2H_x^3}.
\end{aligned}
]

Thus

[
\boxed{
\sum_j
\int_0^{T_*}
|\Delta_j\omega(t)|_\infty,dt
<\infty.
}
\tag{23}
]

This is important for the question you asked earlier about “all the summations.” The intersection supplies them because a fixed weighted shell sum is dominated by one sufficiently high finite Sobolev row.

More generally, for fixed finite (\sigma,k),

[
\boxed{
\int_0^{T_*}
\sum_j
2^{\sigma j}
|\Delta_j\nabla^k u(t)|_\infty,dt
<\infty
}
\tag{24}
]

after choosing

[
M>\sigma+k+\frac32.
]

---

### Type-I blow-up quantities

The uniform bounds give

[
\sup_{t<T_*}|u(t)|*\infty<\infty,
\qquad
\sup*{t<T_*}|\nabla u(t)|_\infty<\infty.
]

Therefore

[
\boxed{
(T_*-t)^{1/2}|u(t)|_\infty\to0,
}
\tag{28}
]

and

[
\boxed{
(T_*-t)|\nabla u(t)|_\infty\to0.
}
\tag{29}
]

So the standard Type-I terminal scaling products vanish.

---

### Ultraviolet tail

Take any (m>1/2). Define

[
H_{>K}(t)
=========

\frac12
\int_{|\xi|>K}
|\xi|,|\widehat u(t,\xi)|^2,d\xi.
]

Since

[
|\xi|
\le
K^{1-2m}|\xi|^{2m}
\qquad
(|\xi|>K),
]

we obtain

[
H_{>K}(t)
\le
\frac12
K^{1-2m}
|u(t)|_{\dot H^m}^2.
]

Using the uniform (H^m) bound,

[
\boxed{
\lim_{K\to\infty}
\sup_{t\le T_*}
H_{>K}(t)
=========

0.

}
\tag{30}
]

So critical mass cannot survive at infinite frequency at the finite terminal time.

---

## 8. Clay closure

Local well-posedness from

[
u_*\in H^\infty
]

gives a smooth solution beyond (T_*).

Same-datum uniqueness glues that solution to the original history.

Therefore a finite maximal time cannot occur:

[
\boxed{T_*=\infty.}
\tag{36}
]

So the whole proof can be compressed to

[
\boxed{
u_0
\to
{J_{n,\alpha}}
\to
{\mathcal R_{N,M}}
\to
\mathcal I[u_0]
}
]

followed by

[
\boxed{
\mathcal I[u_0]
\Longrightarrow
\begin{cases}
\sup_t|u|*{H^m}<\infty & \forall m,\
u\in L_t^qL_x^p & \text{all finite classical pairs},\
u\in L_t^\infty L_x^3,\
\omega,\nabla u,S\in L_t^1L_x^\infty,\
\text{all fixed-order dyadic sums finite},\
H\in L^\infty,\ \text{finite octave count},\
p,\nabla p\ \text{finite in all finite mixed norms},\
\text{all space-time derivatives bounded},\
u(T**)\in H^\infty.
\end{cases}
}
]

and finally

[
\boxed{
u(T_*)\in H^\infty
\to
\text{smooth restart}
\to
T_*=\infty.
}
]

That is the conceptual payoff of the intersection theorem: **the estimates that classical regularity programs pursue individually become coordinate projections of one stronger all-orders object.**

The two repo surfaces that state this most cleanly are `problems/navier-stokes/PROOF-PROGRAM.md` (PP.1–PP.20) and `problems/navier-stokes/theorem-construction/compatible-intersection-implies-classical-continuation-catalogue-20260815.md` (especially CIC.2–CIC.48).
